#{[#edb/ref 17592186050372 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834} :activity/id #uuid "37643965-adaf-5a8f-893a-965115fd6336" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069995 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + {\\color{blue}{a}}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNot all quadratic expressions are perfect squares. However, every quadratic expression can be expressed as a perfect square *plus a constant*. In this lesson, we'll learn how to write quadratic expressions in this form.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 6x + 3^2\\right) + 4\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully written the original expression as a perfect square $(x + {\\color{blue}3})^2$ plus a constant $(4).$\n\nWriting a quadratic expression as a perfect square plus a constant is called **completing the square.** Completing the square is a valuable technique for solving many problems.  \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068672 :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9927" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}-8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068677 :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9997" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+36 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2}-5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069946 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated. \n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}},$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+15 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/math-academy-id "e-10009" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  +4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}}] :activity/title "Completing the Square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068977}] [#edb/ref 17592186050356 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620} :activity/id #uuid "f530f18b-11b2-5bde-8332-6af2fe3c20eb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107074 :tutorial/content "The **scalar triple product** of three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is defined as $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFor example, suppose we have three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 1,1,-2 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle 0,3,1 \\rangle.$ Let's compute their scalar triple product, $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$\n\nFirst, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =7i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨7,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The scalar triple product takes its name from the fact that it multiplies three vectors together and always results in a scalar.\n\nLater, we will learn some geometric intuition behind the scalar triple product. But for now, we will focus on computing the scalar triple product." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107075 :tutorial/content "It turns out that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nThat is to say, if $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's focus on computing the scalar triple product using the determinant above." :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Scalar Triple Product Via the Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107076 :tutorial/content "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n- If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} & 2{a}_{2} & 2{a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n- If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :tutorial/title "Properties of the Scalar Triple Product"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times 3\\mathbf{c}) = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times 3c) & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times 3c)] & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =5 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-\\frac{5}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107077 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Proof of the Formula for the Scalar Triple Product"}}] :activity/title "The Scalar Triple Product" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105963}] [#edb/ref 17592186050343 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130207} :activity/id #uuid "82aa6e24-e2d2-572b-b1aa-bfcb7c8c2948" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130456 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129267 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :question/id #uuid "c6495ab5-3756-50e6-8977-7ec19fba4466" :question/math-academy-id "e-11470" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ for $y \\geq 0$ between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 10 - x.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9a/9ad0d507a5440cfce9595e9054457fd733ae412d9a57b0a85238b4773ff0a0c7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x = 10 - r\\cos\\theta.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 10-r\\cos\\theta \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Cylindrical Solid"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128845 :question/id #uuid "043cc93d-1eae-5640-a109-9acbe7a34f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-11471" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\, : \\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-y \\right\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z = -2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\sin{\\theta}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = 2\\cos{\\theta}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos{\\theta}, \\: -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-r\\sin{\\theta} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128856 :question/id #uuid "a1b2e13a-95cd-5e0f-aa97-c3e90433560c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12402" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x+2y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=5$ for $x\\geq 0,$ $y\\geq 0$ and between the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=y+3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b37ffe8e97ed4a661ee98750894641c6ed3354864dd5b54d2ed076e4fdb69b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+3=r\\sin\\theta + 3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq \\sqrt{5}, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: r\\sin\\theta \\leq z \\leq r\\sin\\theta + 3\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )[z]_{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{z=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )]_{r=0}^{r=\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(\\sqrt{5})}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\sqrt{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}(1-0)-10\\sqrt{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Cylindrical Coordinates"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470 :tutorial/content "Let's now build some intuition behind the meaning of the volume element $\\textrm d V.$ This will help us to understand the change of variables formula in cylindrical polar coordinates. We start by considering the usual rectangular coordinate system.\n\nSuppose we have a point $P$ and a neighboring point $Q$ in the Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below. \n\n![](images/25/2535744585beb7fed89d760c0290a04f54d0a08c8f7e564b17c8e42432da031e.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d x$ parallel to the $x$-axis so that the new point $P'$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we move a distance $\\textrm d y$ parallel to the $y$-axis so that the new point $P''$ has the same $y$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ parallel to the $z$-axis to land to $Q.$\n\nThese movements span the rectangular solid shown above. The volume of this solid, denoted $\\textrm d V,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n$$\n\nThus, the element $\\textrm d V$ represents the volume generated by moving from the point $P$ to the point $Q$ in this coordinate system." :tutorial/title "Deriving the Volume Element in Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130473 :tutorial/content "Let's now repeat this process for a cylindrical polar system. Consider the points $P$ and $Q$ shown below.\n\n![](images/24/243d720546357a33eac3f96b84755cf6aeebafdef3a3672a3d29989147f134c2.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d r$ so that the new point $P'$ has the same $r$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we sweep through an angle $\\textrm d \\theta$ so that the new point $P''$ has the same $\\theta$-coordinate as $Q.$ The arc length traced out by this movement approximately equals $r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ vertically to land on $Q.$\n\nThese three movements trace out a solid that's approximately rectangular, and so the approximate volume of this solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}r \\cdot r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\mathrm{d}z=  r\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\,\\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Deriving the Volume Element in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129389}] [#edb/ref 17592186050412 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172991} :activity/id #uuid "513e69d0-02fb-5684-b1f2-193fc2a3da9b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171891 :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171892 :question/id #uuid "193765c8-ea2c-5d90-b902-09d6d44a3a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9466" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/24/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171893 :question/id #uuid "61c3506a-5e4e-557f-bb8c-077170e93204" :question/math-academy-id "e-9468" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173199 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) is a subset of ![](images/03/03c6559e7c85ee092fae7ca578a2d32203d951ae1f4304ac130ce9084b6542bd.svg)\nA **vector field** on ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) is a function ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) that maps every point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) to a *vector* ![](images/ea/ea8966f11bafff8bb4d89977a7ee41ed5afb424ed0654019c8303a598b186daf.svg) in ![](images/03/03c6559e7c85ee092fae7ca578a2d32203d951ae1f4304ac130ce9084b6542bd.svg)\n\n![](images/5e/5ef5420c0e53b3abe5233a27c1845415d050d5bb95b7ea26b156dfcdea4e6633.png)\n\nFor example, consider the vector field ![](images/e6/e6772ba869702dc10d0028f0feb07f7ce01cc608beb46c5b0576e7c1c9c7d8f5.svg) given by\n![](images/77/77ff19a1027b723d7533c97132b99ae3a5208d29f9878489b7f88cc880ea7365.svg)\n\nThis vector field maps the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) to the vector ![](images/8f/8f0f86eaeae73192e7681a0300e1488e7a4f0893ed43f12cd24a7ef103be1b74.svg) because\n\n![](images/de/de6981eac0d880640d8c140488d434730c6d95b3afdbccc8634c5d586d94855a.svg)\n\nIn general, any vector field ![](images/ea/ea8966f11bafff8bb4d89977a7ee41ed5afb424ed0654019c8303a598b186daf.svg) on ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) can be expressed in terms of its component functions ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) and ![](images/4b/4be111afe7c23428ae5024a8bb9424de4d5b9e8cce74d2258fdffb9bdca9bcfd.svg) as follows:\n![](images/c1/c1362aca3bb7906a1b071716ac3ac330520dfdfde69e72731fb238c63eb80a41.svg)\nIn the example above, we had\n![](images/29/29a41ebbbef1e24712fbb141c3359b4179165b368d17982f56e1f1f1512ba706.svg)\n\n**Note:** We can easily extend our definition of a vector field to three-dimensional or even ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg)-dimensional space. For example, if ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) is a subset of ![](images/55/55a5c860eb76e4bd3419288afaf1ff150cfc7d837cab0048c9ebeb633cf04b0f.svg) then a vector field on ![](images/46/4607eb52aa0195587414ef27616970a6b47226a3bb476e944ac079422dc1e1b0.svg) is a function ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) that maps every point ![](images/0e/0e5197e28e24f43e88f0b3b3a29f2bc1327fc6b730765a62e88d242bfc117b1e.svg) in ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) to a vector ![](images/ec/ec2c632c5fe5848c1f26ac23f4d271bfa5295e86f9b7bd512d215d5ff3844cba.svg) in ![](images/b2/b2854737dd2d2e96422a242158c3b44004d7f327822db17f49758cf92ad880db.svg)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172986}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173200 :tutorial/content "We can find the domain of a vector field by calculating the intersection of the domains of its component functions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhose component functions are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right), \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we find the domain of each component function and then compute their intersection.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-x^2-y^2 \\gt 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2 \\lt 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x^2+y^2 \\lt 4 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is the interior of a circle of radius $2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/0b/0b76751de4305ed2cd3410ea52602756dc05c6d4f5c2a848e2c52220e3a445d1.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-1 \\geq 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x \\geq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is the half-plane shown below:\n\n![](images/88/88dee5adf4ac85267164d42c2256c8ae25dae0ac9e74284e04186ef54c057811.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1 \\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: x^2 + y^2 \\lt 4, \\, x\\geq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe domain of $\\mathbf F$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c7/c7ec35df55bcb910098888ff32cef5566c522c5d469e94409cd96e8762a63a8f.png)" :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172987}}] :activity/title "Vector Fields" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172170}]}
